ss who has been served, indicating potential lien or secondary interest complications. With both primary defendants unserved and residing at-property, this presents a classic pre-judgment acquisition opportunity where owners may be unaware of foreclosure timeline or motivated to avoid judgment. The unserved status suggests either the owners are avoiding process servers or have temporarily relocated, both scenarios indicating financial distress or disengagement from the property.\n\n## Timeline Analysis vs. Benchmarks\n\nTX-26-00991 was filed 05/19/2026 and is currently at 0 months to judgment with both primary defendants unserved. Against HIGH benchmark TX-23-00042 (37 months to judgment, 89 days to OOS), this case is extremely early-stage with approximately 37 months remaining before forced sale. Against LOW benchmark TX-25-00492 (14 months to judgment), we still have 14+ months of runway. The unserved status on both primary defendants will significantly delay proceedings—service attempts, potential substitute service motions, and possible citation by publication could add 4-6 months alone. Judge Ginsberg''s docket typically runs 18-24 months to default judgment on uncontested cases. **Conservative timeline estimate: 16-20 months to judgment, 22-26 months to OOS.** This extended timeline favors negotiated acquisition with substantial equity opportunity before legal pressure intensifies.\n\n## Acquisition Strategy\n\n**Contact Method:** Door-knock campaign FIRST (3 attempts: weekday evening, Saturday morning, Sunday afternoon) given owners'' residence at property and unserved status—personal contact may catch them off-guard and establish rapport before they retain counsel. Follow with direct mail sequence if door-knocks unsuccessful: (1) handwritten envelope "Time-Sensitive Property Matter", (2) typed letter 7 days later, (3) yellow "Final Notice" letter 10 days after. **','\n',char(10)),NULL,'pre_judgment',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-01T23:56:27.338518','2026-07-0