y 6 months ago (05/19/2026), Martinez is likely facing financial pressure from mounting legal costs and the uncertainty of prolonged litigation, but has not yet experienced the full severity of a judgment or foreclosure timeline.\n\n## Timeline Analysis vs. Benchmarks\n\nTX-26-00992 is currently at approximately 6 months from filing with no judgment date set, tracking significantly slower than benchmark TX-25-00492 (LOW priority, 14mo→J) but well ahead of the concerning TX-23-00042 timeline (HIGH priority, 37mo→J, 89d→OOS). Given Judge Ginsberg''s docket and the Answer filing, we anticipate judgment within 8-12 months (Q1-Q2 2027), placing total timeline at 14-18 months to judgment. This positions us in an optimal acquisition window—Martinez has experienced enough litigation stress to be motivated but hasn''t yet faced judgment, wage garnishment, or sheriff sale scheduling. The 6-month mark represents the "sweet spot" where owners recognize the problem''s permanence but haven''t exhausted all emotional and financial resources fighting it.\n\n## Acquisition Strategy\n\n**Initial Contact Method:** Door knock primary residence (209 W. Ridgewood) during evening hours (6-8 PM) Tuesday-Thursday to establish personal rapport and assess financial distress indicators. Follow up with direct mail campaign (handwritten envelope, personalized letter) if no contact after 3 attempts over 2 weeks.\n\n**Offer Range:** $145,000-$165,000 depending on property condition, equity position, and debt stack with Resolution Finance. Structure as cash close within 21 days, with assumption of legal case dismissal costs (estimate $2,500-$5,000 attorney fees) as additional negotiating leverage. Emphasize "stop the lawsuit, walk away clean" positioning rather than equity discussion.\n\n**Key','\n',char(10)),NULL,'pre_judgment',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-01T23:57:12.499163','2026-07-05T22:35:40.182685','2026-07-04T20:02:11.064121',1,'individual','high','',NULL);
